<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 12+ system splash icon. Replaces Cordova's stock hardhat robot
  (drawable/ic_cdv_splashscreen.xml) via the AndroidWindowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon
  preference in config.xml, which copies this file over the generated one on
  every `cordova prepare`.

  This is the same sigil the web opening draws, on the same 400 unit viewBox and
  the same coordinates, so the native splash reads as the first frame of the
  opening rather than a different picture handing over to it. That includes the
  twelve zodiac glyphs: they are the sigil, and a ring without them reads as a
  compass rose.

  Three constraints shape the numbers:

    - The platform masks the animated icon to a circle and only reveals the
      inner two thirds of the canvas. The group below scales the artwork to
      0.66 about the centre, which pulls the r=190 rim in to r=125, inside the
      r=133 the mask actually shows.
    - The glyphs sit at r=158 in the lane between the wheel's r=178 and r=138
      circles, exactly as in www/index.html. Scaled 1.6 they span r=139 to
      r=177, which keeps them inside that lane.
    - The colour is Sanctuary Dark's gold token, hardcoded because the native
      layer cannot read a CSS custom property. Keep it in step with the gold in
      www/css/theme.css. The background behind it is cdv_splashscreen_background
      (see res/android/values*/cdv_colors.xml), and gold carries on both the
      cream and the dark ground, so one icon serves light and dark alike.

  Deliberately NOT logo.png: that asset bakes in its own dark rounded square
  plate, which the circle mask would render as a black disc on the cream ground.

  Note for editors: the glyph groups below are GENERATED from
  ZODIAC_GLYPH_PATHS in src/splash.ts, which is why every path here is arc only
  and matches the web copies character for character. Those copies are
  www/index.html and www/profile.html; four in total, and tests/splash.test.ts
  fails if they drift. Edit the constant, not this file.

  Note for editors: no stroke-dasharray here. Android VectorDrawable does not
  support it, so the dashed ring in the web sigil is simply omitted.

  Note for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment.
-->
